  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1044 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe weather starts to become more interesting by Tuesday afternoon. \nSPC has continued the slight risk of severe weather over the central \nand eastern panhandles Tuesday. Models showing pieces of energy from \nthe remnants of former Hurricane Simon moving into west TX\nTuesday. The associated subtle shortwave in this southwesterly \nflow will be moving across the area Tuesday afternoon/evening. \nThis seems like the most favored time for convective initiation. \nThe RRFS is showing thunderstorms developing just to the northeast\nand east of AMA at this time. NAM forecast soundings showing CAPE\nvalues of 1500-2000 j/kg late Tuesday afternoon over mainly the\nsouthern and eastern panhandle areas. Will need to monitor this \nactivity for potential severe weather\n\nThe main upper low..currently over central Washington..is still \nslowly digging south and by 12Z Wednesday should be near the 4 \ncorners area according to the NAM. This is a little further south \nthan yesterday and places the jet max closer to the area which will \nprovide more of an impact than just skirting by as it appeared \nyesterday. Also with the slower positioning of the upper low the \ndryline will still likely be west of the area on Wednesday and \ntherefore pops are still moderately high across most of the central \nand eastern panhandles into Wednesday night and then being\nconfined to the southeast Thursday morning. \n\nAfter the upper low moves northeast, enters the plains and moves \naway from the area much cooler weather is expected with highs in \nthe lower 60s expected for Friday and Saturday. Overnight lows \nwill see upper 30s across the northwest with lows in the low to \nmid 40s elsewhere.",
